  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_94f7295dbcce46cb93b2185f9b344f90",
      "statement": "MovieCollectionManager's core components are mcm-app (universal Expo/React Native frontend fronted by the BFF), mc-service (Rust/Axum service owning all movie-collection domain logic and the sole writer to mc-db), mc-db (a single shared MongoDB database mc_db with movie_collections and movies collections), and Keycloak as the external IAM provider expecting a movie-collection-manager client with mc-admin/mc-user client roles (new self-registrations default to mc-user).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/MCM-Architecture.md#L24-L35",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7e2fb252bb101e20539e85a1efb5b99531e8db5477b0c99e9ea43e4949676ce8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_7909366289a84dac80e620165d50ed05",
      "statement": "Access control in MCM is split into two independent layers: RBAC (Keycloak client roles mc-admin vs mc-user, enforced before a request reaches domain logic) and DAC (per-collection owner/contributor/viewer rights recorded in each collection's own acl array in movie_collections, enforced inside mc-service, with no notion of individual collections at the Keycloak layer).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/MCM-Architecture.md#L54-L69",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f1d97a7dc39f403494f84cbadf5958275cf54da6f884219240cd0d8f73fa3686:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_380814634df047129c8c7da88a9852ed",
      "statement": "Feature 073 adds a per-user scheduled/on-demand backup capability run in-process by the BFF: it copies a user's own collections to an S3-compatible or WebDAV destination the user owns and supplies, retains the last N versions, and restores only by creating new collections (never overwriting live data); unattended runs authenticate via a Keycloak offline token the user explicitly consented to, and destination secrets are sealed under their own encryption key (BACKUP_CREDENTIAL_ENC_KEY), separate from the agent-config encryption key (AGENT_CONFIG_ENC_KEY). This is a BFF-owned capability, not part of mc-service or mc-db.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/MCM-Architecture.md#L31-L31",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:88a9ad89b28d2ea58c15dbd305e9dad0ce2563d0b5c77d27c93bcf33cbff0bb5:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://specs/073-scheduled-backups/spec.md#L1-L24",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:8fa163c5ac1668b9044daa376df2d94f9c9a0591ad0d47b35d506b8c1bde7e3a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_caf2598e6cf245a4b127e2700d28fe75",
      "statement": "Grant/revoke of contributor/viewer DAC rights is not yet built in the product: mc-service authorizes against the acl array and tests populate non-owner roles, but no UI or endpoint exists to actually grant or revoke a contributor/viewer entry, so every real collection's acl today contains only the owner entry.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/MCM-Architecture.md#L92-L92",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6b97456b4bf394af62a226fc485f0b71c2e67d90dffa8d8f41f8f4d172b41b68:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_41691b2e4a8d49e28614fc75aa897d2a",
      "statement": "The AI Agents layer (Agent Gateway, movie-mcp, movie-assistant) is additive: mc-service and existing mcm-app screens are unchanged, and every agent-originated call to mc-service is routed through movie-mcp carrying a downscoped, audience-bound user token, so the existing RBAC (mc-admin/mc-user) and DAC (owner/contributor/viewer) enforcement applies unchanged rather than being bypassed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/MCM-Architecture.md#L113-L125",
